+scan. A table with indexes may have several, and exactly one of them is used:
+
+| Path | Serves | From |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| lookup | `col = <literal>`, `col IN (...)`, `col IS NULL` | a hash index, or a sorted index for the first two |
+| range | every `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` conjunct on one column, folded into one interval | a sorted index |
+| ordered | a single `ORDER BY` key on the indexed column | a sorted index |
+
+Ranking is lookup, then range, then ordered, and ties go to the column that
+comes first in the table's schema, so the choice never depends on the order
+the predicates were written in. Conjuncts the path does not answer stay on the
+operator and are applied to each fetched row.
+
+An ordered path removes the `Sort` above it rather than feeding it, which is
+the only case where the access path changes the operator tree above the scan.
+The sort key is followed down through projections that only rename columns,
+because `ORDER BY` on a column outside the select list is projected as a
+hidden column first.
+
+An index changes no answer. Offsets come out of an index grouped by key, so
+they are sorted back into table order before the rows are fetched, and a
+descending ordered read reverses keys without disturbing rows that share one.
+The visible difference is `rows_scanned`, which counts the rows an access path
+really reads.
